lisa-design:
- Intake order: rules.CouldFire first, the link lookup only when no rule could fire, so the sync-on-empty costs a GET only before a drop (P3: "syncs ... before it drops anything").
- One lookup, linkedWorkItem (rule.go), shared by workItemFor and the intake filter; its sync-on-empty applies to both (P3 §Reads and freshness names both callers).
- The sync goes through an unexported storeSyncer interface + syncIfSupported in internal/controllers/storesync.go, because PR 1 (store.Syncer / store.SyncIfSupported) is built in parallel. Switch to store.SyncIfSupported when restacked onto PR 1.
- Drop reasons: "no-rule" (no rule's on+providers take the type from the provider) and "out-of-scope" (some do, but their RuleSets' scope doesn't hold the object). Computed from the catalog's public fields in controllers, not by changing internal/rules (CAT-owned).
- Drops are logged/counted only after the delivery's outcome is written (update or delete), so a retried reconcile doesn't double count.
- factory_events_dropped_total is fed through server.Options.Intake (a DropCounter interface the ProviderController implements), counts since process start, only label sets seen are rendered, like factory_http_requests_total.
- Unmapped types (no events) are not counted as drops: there is no event to count; the delivery is just deleted.

lisa-design:
- worker.Options.Poll is a value struct (Wait, Interval) with no default: its zero Wait means zero, and the zero Poll is refused by New as a hot loop. worker.DefaultPoll() is the long poll. Rejected: a *Poll with nil meaning the default, which would put the "zero means unset" ambiguity back one level up.
- The 30s default lives in the config only (poll.wait absent -> "30s"); the config keeps the strings as written (Poll.Wait/Interval) and exposes WaitDuration/IntervalDuration, like WorkspaceSize.MemoryGiB.
- The config and the worker both check the poll (whole-second wait 0s-60s, non-negative interval, wait 0s needs interval >= 1s): config for the owner's message, worker.New so no caller can hot-loop. config does not import worker (it stays a leaf).
- Only a 204 sleeps the interval, with the slot released and on loopCtx (drain and abort end it). A lease, a failure (backoff) and a throttle keep their own paths.
- poll.interval has no upper bound and no whole-second rule (P3 sets neither); a long poll may carry an interval.
